(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_electromagneticwaves=[{id:"neet-2026r-q005",pn:5,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"EM wave in a dielectric medium",q:"An electromagnetic wave travelling in a lossless dielectric medium having a dielectric constant, \u03B5\u1D63 = 9, has the electric field, E\u2093 = E\u2080 sin (kz \u2013 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076t) Vm\u207B\xB9 where E\u2080 is the amplitude and k is the wave vector. Among the following options, the incorrect choice is",o:["The wavelength of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 300 m","The magnetic field is given by the relation B_y = (B\u2080/v) sin(kz \u2013 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076t) where v is the speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium","The direction of propagation of the electromagnetic wave is along +z","The speed of the electromagnetic wave inside the medium is 10\u2078 ms\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`E\u2093 = E\u2080 sin(kz \u2013 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076t) = E\u2080 sin(kz \u2013 \u03C9t).
So the wave travels along +z.
Speed in the medium: v\u2098 = v\u2080/\u221A\u03B5\u1D63 = c/\u221A9 = (3 \xD7 10\u2078)/3 = 10\u2078 m/s.
Frequency f\u2080 = \u03C9/2\u03C0 = 10\u2076 Hz, and it stays constant.
Wavelength in the medium: \u03BB\u2098 = v\u2098/f\u2080 = 10\u2078/(2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u2076/2\u03C0) = 100 m.
So the wavelength inside the medium is 100 m, not 300 m, and option (1) is incorrect.
Note: in option (2), B\u2080 should be replaced by E\u2080.
Note: Option (2) as printed uses B\u2080 where E\u2080 is meant (publisher notes this); answer (1) is still the incorrect statement`},{id:"neet-2026r-q018",pn:18,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"EM spectrum applications",q:`The following table presents the part of the electromagnetic spectrum and their corresponding major applications.
P. Microwave \u2014 I. For purifying the water
Q. UV rays \u2014 II. For warming the food
R. Gamma rays \u2014 III. For AM and FM communication systems
S. Radio wave \u2014 IV. For treating the Cancer cells
The correct option is:`,o:["P-I, Q-IV, R-II, S-III","P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III","P-II, Q-IV, R-III, S-I","P-I, Q-II, R-III, S-IV"],c:1,s:`Microwave is used for warming food.
UV rays are used for purifying water.
Gamma rays are used for treating cancer cells.
Radio waves are used for AM and FM communication systems.
So P-II, Q-I, R-IV, S-III.`},{id:"neet-2026-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Production of EM waves",q:`Match List I with List II:
List-I (Electromagnetic wave) \u2014 List-II (Production)
A. Microwave \u2014 I. Electrons in atoms emit light when they move from a higher energy level to a lower energy level
B. Visible light \u2014 II. Radioactive decay of nucleus
C. Gamma rays \u2014 III. Vibration of atoms and molecules
D. Infra-red rays \u2014 IV. Klystron valve or magnetron valve
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-I, C-II, D-III"],c:3,s:`Microwaves are produced by a klystron valve or magnetron valve: A-IV.
Visible light comes from electrons in atoms moving from a higher to a lower energy level: B-I.
Gamma rays come from radioactive decay of the nucleus: C-II.
Infra-red rays come from vibration of atoms and molecules: D-III.`},{id:"neet-2025-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Relation between E and B in EM wave",q:`The electric field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by
E_z = 60 cos (5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t) V/m.
Then expression for the corresponding magnetic field is (here subscripts denote the direction of the field) :`,o:["B_y = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 cos (5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t)T","B_x = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 cos (5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t)T","B_z = 60cos (5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t)T","B_y = 60 sin (5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t)T"],c:0,s:`In an electromagnetic wave, E and B are in the same phase and B\u2080 = E\u2080/c; their directions are perpendicular to each other.
\u2234 B_y = (60/c) cos(5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t) T
= (60/(3 \xD7 10\u2078)) cos(5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t) T
B_y = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 cos(5x + 1.5 \xD7 10\u2079 t) T`},{id:"neet-2025-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Displacement current",q:"A parallel plate capacitor made of circular plates is being charged such that the surface charge density on its plates is increasing at a constant rate with time. The magnetic field arising due to displacement current is:",o:["Zero at all places","Constant between the plates and zero outside the plates","Non-zero everywhere with maximum at the imaginary cylindrical surface connecting peripheries of the plates","Zero between the plates and non-zero outside"],c:2,s:`Let the surface charge density be \u03C3 = q/A.
Given d\u03C3/dt = constant.
So d/dt (q/A) = constant, which gives i/A = constant (i is the current, I/A in the print).
It means the displacement current is constant.
This system acts like a cylindrical wire.
The graph of magnetic field B vs r is shown: B rises linearly with r inside, is maximum at the edge, then falls outside.`,sfig:{w:200,h:155,alt:"Graph of B against r: straight rise from the origin to a peak, then a falling curve that flattens towards the r-axis",d:[["l",20,137,20,7,"a"],["l",20,137,185,137,"a"],["t",30,18,"B"],["t",190,145,"r"],["p","M20 137 L100 60 C115 95 140 120 177 123","B"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q037",pn:37,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Displacement current",q:"A parallel plate capacitor is charged by connecting it to a battery through a resistor. If I is the current in the circuit, then in the gap between the plates;",o:["displacement current of magnitude equal to I flows in a direction opposite to that of I.","displacement current of magnitude greater than I flows but can be in any direction.","there is no current.","displacement current of magnitude equal to I flows in the same direction as I."],c:3,s:`Use the modified Ampere's law: \u222EB\xB7d\u2113 = \u03BC\u2080(I_C + I_D).
Loop L1 is around the wire: I_C \u2260 0 and I_D = 0.
Loop L2 is in the gap between the plates: I_C = 0 and I_D \u2260 0.
By current continuity (like KCL), I_C = I_D.
So the displacement current equals I and has the same direction.`,sfig:{w:230,h:100,alt:"A capacitor with two vertical plates; a wire with a rightward arrow reaches the left plate and a wire leaves the right plate. Dashed loop L1 encircles the wire left of the left plate and dashed loop L2 lies in the gap between the plates.",d:[["l",3,50,38,50],["l",38,50,48,50,"a"],["l",48,50,57,50],["l",57,17,57,83],["l",143,17,143,83],["l",143,50,223,50],["p","M43 17 A14 33 0 1 0 43 83 A14 33 0 1 0 43 17 Z","d"],["p","M112 17 A15 33 0 1 0 112 83 A15 33 0 1 0 112 17 Z","d"],["t",36,11,"L\u2081"],["t",104,11,"L\u2082"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q038",pn:38,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Properties of EM waves",q:"The property which is not of an electromagnetic wave travelling in free space is that;",o:["they travel with a speed equal to 1/\u221A(\u03BC\u2080\u03B5\u2080)","they originate from charges moving with uniform speed.","they are transverse in nature.","they energy density in electric field is equal to energy density in magnetic field."],c:1,s:`EM waves come from an accelerating charge.
A charge moving with uniform velocity gives only a steady magnetic field, not a wave.`},{id:"neet-2023-q031",pn:31,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Relation between E0 and B0",q:"In a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in free space, the electric field component oscillates sinusoidally at a frequency of 2.0 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070 Hz and amplitude 48 V m\u207B\xB9. Then the amplitude of oscillating magnetic field is : (Speed of light in free space = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m s\u207B\xB9)",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 T","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 T","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T"],c:3,s:`For an electromagnetic wave, c = E\u2080/B\u2080.
E\u2080 is the electric field amplitude and B\u2080 is the magnetic field amplitude.
B\u2080 = 48/(3\xD710\u2078) = 1.6\xD710\u207B\u2077 T.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q010",pn:10,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Electric and magnetic field in EM wave",q:"The magnetic field of a plane electromagnetic wave is given by B = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 cos(1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x + 48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t) \u0135, then the associated electric field will be:",o:["9cos(1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x + 48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t) k\u0302 V/m","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 cos(1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x + 48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t) \xEE V/m","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 sin(1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x + 48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t) \xEE V/m","9sin(1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x \u2212 48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t) k\u0302 V/m"],c:0,s:`E\u2080/B\u2080 = c, so E\u2080 = B\u2080c = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u2078 = 9 V/m.
E and B are in the same phase in a travelling EM wave, so the cosine with the same argument is kept.
The wave travels along \u2212x (because of +48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t with 1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x). The direction of E is given by \xCA = B\u0302 \xD7 \u0109 = \u0135 \xD7 (\u2212\xEE) = k\u0302.
So E = 9cos(1.6 \xD7 10\xB3x + 48 \xD7 10\xB9\u2070t) k\u0302 V/m.`},{id:"neet-2022-q013",pn:13,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Speed of light in a medium",q:"When light propagates through a material medium of relative permittivity \u03B5r and relative permeability \u03BCr, the velocity of light, v is given by (c-velocity of light in vacuum)",o:["v = \u221A(\u03BCr/\u03B5r)","v = \u221A(\u03B5r/\u03BCr)","v = c/\u221A(\u03B5r\u03BCr)","v = c"],c:2,s:`In a medium v = 1/\u221A(\u03B5\u03BC) = 1/\u221A(\u03B5\u2080\u03B5r \u03BC\u2080\u03BCr).
In vacuum c = 1/\u221A(\u03B5\u2080\u03BC\u2080).
So v = c/\u221A(\u03B5r \u03BCr).`},{id:"neet-2022r-q022",pn:22,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Ratio of B and E in EM wave",q:"The ratio of the magnitude of the magnetic field and electric field intensity of a plane electromagnetic wave in free space of permeability \u03BC\u2080 and permittivity \u03B5\u2080 is (Given that C-velocity of light in free space)",o:["(\u221A(\u03BC\u2080\u03B5\u2080))/(c)","c","1/c","c/(\u221A(\u03BC\u2080\u03B5\u2080))"],c:2,s:`In an EM wave |E| = c|B|.
So |B|/|E| = 1/c.`},{id:"neet-2022-q035",pn:35,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Electromagnetic Waves",st:"Wavelengths of EM spectrum",q:"Match List-I with List-II. List-I (Electromagnetic waves): (a) AM radio waves (b) Microwaves (c) Infrared radiations (d) X-rays. List-II (Wavelength): (i) 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m (ii) 10\xB2 m (iii) 10\u207B\xB2 m (iv) 10\u207B\u2074 m. Choose the correct answer from the options given below",o:["(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (iv), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (iii), (c) - (iv), (d) - (i)","(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)"],c:2,s:`AM radio waves have wavelength about 10\xB2 m.
Microwaves have about 10\u207B\xB2 m.
Infrared radiations have about 10\u207B\u2074 m.
X-rays have about 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 m.
So (a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i).`}]})();
